#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f263a71f-cb08-5ab4-8b39-55586d9ea99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381099} {:answer/id #uuid "9744f481-5ae1-5231-9a09-e247bbdc4593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381100} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9db714-7d99-5035-9114-dd0a525d26d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381101} {:answer/id #uuid "71d989e1-88a2-53df-a913-f1634e3648d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381102} {:answer/id #uuid "37555c97-92eb-5239-9d15-5377332b0503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9db714-7d99-5035-9114-dd0a525d26d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381101} :answer-field/id #uuid "813ed7fd-ec6e-5ac9-bae1-d7c428faa03d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29923499-4636-5ade-b9ad-4388f4415532" :question/math-academy-id "q-139442" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,(A+2I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+2I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A+2I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+2I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+2I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A+2I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A+2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+2I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+2I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b35dce-f075-54ec-ba79-7120e2d404cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399066} {:answer/id #uuid "40cf39a1-7eb8-57bc-a91b-26c7b8930628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399067} {:answer/id #uuid "61db48ee-4b64-5a58-b44e-1d5c9b96cb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399068} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce29f2e-6690-5c30-addf-eb5b0269e1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399069} {:answer/id #uuid "600532cc-f1be-58a0-bec6-3ac60d7f098d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cf39a1-7eb8-57bc-a91b-26c7b8930628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399067} :answer-field/id #uuid "4257f8e5-da5d-58bc-8b61-523a29fc6e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "775b4967-50a1-57a8-aeb1-f88b0999b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-78500" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2bbdd76-6c28-529e-93aa-9c2d32cfcca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175559} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c3bdd-0c01-51b5-ab08-24122bea76d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175560} {:answer/id #uuid "06b6607a-5d73-5017-8a01-9b4dc5457949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175561} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87c5e8-8384-57d9-918b-e9f1dd1c836b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 